Not with 'at', rather with 'batch', as shown upthread in the list.
The 'batch' command, according to the man page, supports features of
batch processing.
If I read "batch" I first associate 'batch' (as in "batch processing",
see below). And only if that doesn't make any sense as '*.bat'. YMMV.

But "batch" is not only the Unix program but also a concept, as said;
"batch processing" (http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Batch_processing).
If you read carefully you will see that its central properties are
a) to run "without manual intervention"
b) "in contrast to interactive programs"
c) and "background execution"
Those properties, as I mentioned in my very first posting, make hardly
sense with Nethack.
While an MS bat file can be restrictively written to comply as control
input data to those properties, it is not granted; because the bat file
syntax in general, and the specific demands of the OP, as reflected in
the script that had later been provided, are clearly interactive;
interactively reading in some choices, finally firing the executable
which operated also interactive. A real batch file would run a job
detached in time, and considering system workload.
The MS *.bat file is a misnomer. Folks who don't know batch processing
may associate that alone if they hear "batch". On WinDOS it's primarily
not the bat files (and their command interpreters) that are usable for
batch processing, rather a job scheduler (like cron, a primitive one,
on Unixes).

